[{"_1":2,"_3":4},"root",{"_5":107},"en/routes/conteudo-do-cubo.tsx",{"_5":6},"data",{"_7":8,"_9":10,"_11":12,"_13":14,"_15":-7,"_16":17,"_18":19,"_20":-7,"_21":22,"_23":24},"tipo","metodo","cubo",{"_11":105,"_13":105,"_27":106},"id","reducao","nome","Reduction","nomeLongo","termo",{"_27":102,"_103":104},"niveis",[52],"introducao","etapas",[31,32,33],"historias",[25],{"_13":26,"_27":28,"_29":30},"The 5x5 and reduction","endereco","/historia#5x5","resumo","The 5x5 (Professor's Cube) was created by Udo Krell in 1981 and made by Uwe Mèffert from 1983.",{"_11":73,"_13":74,"_36":75,"_38":76,"_40":77,"_41":78},{"_11":42,"_13":43,"_36":44,"_38":45,"_40":46,"_41":47},{"_11":34,"_13":35,"_36":37,"_38":39,"_40":-7,"_41":-7},"3x3","Like a 3x3","html","<p>With centers and edges done, solve it like a 3x3, turning only the outer faces (<code>R</code>, <code>U</code>, <code>F</code>…): the <a href=\"/en/3x3/beginner\">3x3 beginner method</a> or <a href=\"/en/glossary#cfop\" class=\"termo-do-glossario\" title=\"The most used method in competitions: the cross, the 4 F2L pairs, OLL (makes the top face one color) and PLL (puts the top pieces in place).\">CFOP</a> work just the same. Don&#39;t turn the inner layers: they break up the centers and the edges.</p>\n","grupos",[],"pagina","treino","arestas","Edges","<p>Join the 3 pieces of each edge; the last 2 have cases (<a href=\"/en/glossary#l2e\" class=\"termo-do-glossario\" title=\"In the 5x5 reduction: joins the pieces of the last 2 edges, finishing the reduction.\">L2E</a>). Then, solve it like a 3x3: on the 5x5 there is no <a href=\"/en/glossary#paridade\" class=\"termo-do-glossario\" title=\"On the 4x4 (and even cubes), situations that don't exist on the 3x3: a single flipped edge (OLL Parity) or two swapped pieces (PLL Parity).…\">parity</a> in that part.</p>\n",[49],{"_13":43,"_27":48},"/5x5/treino?grupo=l2e","/5x5/arestas",{"_11":50,"_13":50,"_51":52,"_53":54,"_55":56,"_57":58,"_27":59},"L2E","nivel","intermediario","casos",13,"conjunto",false,"ids",[60,61,62,63,64,65,66,67,68,69,70,71,72],"/5x5/l2e","5X5-L2E1","5X5-L2E2","5X5-L2E3","5X5-L2E4","5X5-L2E5","5X5-L2E6","5X5-L2E7","5X5-L2E8","5X5-L2E9","5X5-L2E10","5X5-L2E11","5X5-L2E12","5X5-L2E13","centros","Centers","<p>Build the centers (9 pieces each). The first four are intuitive; the last 2 have cases (<a href=\"/en/glossary#l2c\" class=\"termo-do-glossario\" title=\"In the 5x5 reduction: builds the last 2 centers (the top and front ones).\">L2C</a>).</p>\n",[80],{"_13":74,"_27":79},"/5x5/treino?grupo=l2c","/5x5/centros",{"_11":81,"_13":81,"_51":52,"_53":82,"_55":56,"_57":83,"_27":84},"L2C",17,[85,86,87,88,89,90,91,92,93,94,95,96,97,98,99,100,101],"/5x5/l2c","5X5-L2C1","5X5-L2C2","5X5-L2C3","5X5-L2C4","5X5-L2C5","5X5-L2C6","5X5-L2C7","5X5-L2C8","5X5-L2C9","5X5-L2C10","5X5-L2C11","5X5-L2C12","5X5-L2C13","5X5-L2C14","5X5-L2C16","5X5-L2C17","5X5-L2C18","/en/glossary#reducao","dica","For 4x4 and 5x5: build the centers and pair up the edges until the cube is like a 3x3, which is solved the usual way.","5x5","/5x5/",{"_108":109,"_110":111},"cubos",[114,115,116,117,118,119],"idiomas",[112,113],"pt","en",{"_11":34,"_13":34},{"_11":125,"_13":125},{"_11":124,"_13":124},{"_11":105,"_13":105},{"_11":122,"_13":123},{"_11":120,"_13":121},"pyraminx","Pyraminx","megaminx","Megaminx","4x4","2x2"]
